  "body": "Build one shared pure-Rust inverse kinematics solver for table-table XYZAC machines inside `crates/post` so both the machine checker and posts compute the same coordinates.\n\n### Context\n- `crates/post/src/post/mod.rs` writes canonical `[A, B, 0]` angles from `cl::axis_to_angles` (`crates/cl/src/cl/pose.rs`), with no AC trunnion kinematics.\n- On a non-RTCP table-table machine, tool length stays parallel to machine `Z`, while `A` and `C` rotate the workpiece around `pivot` and child `offset` vectors.\n\n### Requirements\n- Implement forward and inverse kinematics using Rodrigues axis-angle rotation for unit rotary vectors and non-zero `A` pivot and `C`-to-`A` offsets.\n- Evaluate both analytical branches (`(+A, C)` and `(-A, C + 180 deg)`) against `A.travel` and `C.travel`, and refuse unreachable moves by name.\n- Handle `A` near `0` (pole) by holding the previous modal `C` angle instead of snapping `C` to `0`.\n- Minimize weighted angular steps across moves to cross `A = 0` smoothly when travel permits, instead of causing 180-degree `C` flips during cuts.\n- Unwrap continuous `C` angles inside a simultaneous section within finite `C` limits, and support re-wrapping only at safe retract boundaries.\n- Subdivide linear moves whose angular step exceeds chord tolerance so curved rotary motion keeps tool-tip accuracy.\n\nDone means: Unit tests verify round-trip FK/IK (`<= 0.001 mm` tip error) with non-zero offsets, asymmetric `A` limits, smooth pole crossings, continuous `C` unwrapping, and named refusal for unreachable tool orientations.\n\nPart of #1958\n",
